WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.510
Here is the normal physiology.

00:00:01.925 --> 00:00:05.332
Adrenaline binds the beta-adrenergic receptor of
a heart muscle cell.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.551
The receptor switches on a G protein, and cyclic
AMP is made.

00:00:10.058 --> 00:00:13.022
If the hormone stays bound, the response fades
within minutes.

00:00:13.794 --> 00:00:18.328
Robert Lefkowitz at Duke University had studied
this fading since the start of his career.

00:00:19.022 --> 00:00:22.746
It was known that phosphate is added to the
receptor as the response fades.

00:00:23.331 --> 00:00:29.516
The obvious candidate was protein kinase A, the
kinase that cyclic AMP itself switches on.

00:00:30.336 --> 00:00:37.699
Jeffrey Benovic, a graduate student in the
Lefkowitz laboratory, purified a protein kinase
from cow brain cortex, 100 g at a time.

00:00:38.168 --> 00:00:39.926
Each column made the enzyme purer.

00:00:40.404 --> 00:00:43.940
He enriched it 20,000 times, to more than 90%
purity.

00:00:44.512 --> 00:00:46.459
The pure kinase still did its chemistry.

00:00:46.753 --> 00:00:51.844
It was tested in a tube with purified
beta-adrenergic receptor and radioactive ATP.

00:00:52.353 --> 00:00:57.173
It added 3 to 4 phosphates to every receptor
molecule, just as the crude material had.

00:00:57.727 --> 00:01:01.706
But the purer the kinase became, the less it
switched the receptor off.

00:01:02.376 --> 00:01:09.450
A batch purified 350 times had reduced the
receptor's ability to activate its G protein by
up to 80%.

00:01:10.023 --> 00:01:12.626
The pure enzyme reduced it by only 10 to 15%.

00:01:13.054 --> 00:01:17.941
Protein kinase A was the obvious candidate, so
Benovic first had to rule it out.

00:01:18.443 --> 00:01:20.074
He used cells that cannot make it.

00:01:20.688 --> 00:01:25.788
The kin⁻ line of S49 mouse lymphoma cells has no
working protein kinase A.

00:01:26.472 --> 00:01:36.225
He mixed three things: a high-speed supernatant
from broken kin⁻ cells, hamster lung receptor
rebuilt into lipid vesicles, and radioactive
ATP.

00:01:36.903 --> 00:01:43.105
In the first successful experiment,
phosphorylation increased when the agonist
isoproterenol was present.

00:01:43.801 --> 00:01:46.585
The antagonist alprenolol blocked this increase.

00:01:47.108 --> 00:01:52.988
So phosphate went onto the receptor only when a
hormone was in the binding pocket, and only onto
serines.

00:01:53.525 --> 00:01:56.120
The kinase recognized the receptor's active
shape.

00:01:56.723 --> 00:02:02.278
So the purification had been removing a second
protein, and that protein came from studies of
vision.

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:08.242
Hermann Kühn, at Jülich in West Germany, had
described an abundant protein in the retina.

00:02:08.866 --> 00:02:11.371
It was then called the 48K protein.

00:02:11.932 --> 00:02:15.264
It worked with rhodopsin kinase to switch
rhodopsin off.

00:02:15.872 --> 00:02:21.880
The protein was later renamed arrestin, because
it "arrested" (stopped) rhodopsin function.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:27.499
Lefkowitz says that he telephoned Kühn and
arranged for a shipment of the protein to Duke.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.410
You work in Robert Lefkowitz's laboratory at
Duke.

00:02:31.014 --> 00:02:35.212
Your pure kinase adds phosphates to the receptor
only while an agonist is bound.

00:02:35.812 --> 00:02:38.156
You also have the retinal protein that Kühn
sent.

00:02:38.735 --> 00:02:44.099
Today you will add both to the same preparation,
which holds the receptor and Gs in lipid
vesicles.

00:02:44.718 --> 00:02:47.300
Before you run the experiment, design your
controls.

00:02:47.848 --> 00:02:57.477
The controls must let you say whether a change
in G-protein activation needed the mark (the
phosphates), the reader (the retinal protein),
or both.

00:02:57.854 --> 00:02:58.842
Answer two questions.

00:02:59.523 --> 00:03:01.189
1. Which controls will you run?

00:03:01.771 --> 00:03:04.018
2. What do you predict each control will show?

00:03:04.386 --> 00:03:12.606
In the reconstituted system, the pure kinase put
about 8 phosphates on each receptor, not 3 or 4
as in the earlier tube experiments.

00:03:13.161 --> 00:03:15.451
The marked receptor could still switch on Gs.

00:03:15.989 --> 00:03:18.323
Then Benovic added the retinal protein as well.

00:03:18.797 --> 00:03:22.686
Now the receptor lost about 40% of its ability
to switch on Gs.

00:03:23.212 --> 00:03:24.643
The controls show the mechanism.

00:03:25.323 --> 00:03:30.602
1. Bovine serum albumin, an unrelated protein,
could not replace arrestin.

00:03:31.429 --> 00:03:34.968
2. Heat-denatured arrestin could not replace it
either.

00:03:35.752 --> 00:03:40.456
3. Arrestin added to a receptor that the kinase
had never marked did nothing.

00:03:41.066 --> 00:03:46.415
Activity stayed at 98.1% of the value without
arrestin, across 13 measurements.

00:03:46.916 --> 00:03:51.947
So 8 phosphates could be on the receptor's tail
while the receptor still switched on its G
protein.

00:03:52.355 --> 00:03:56.158
The protein that bound those phosphates was what
switched the receptor off.

00:03:56.654 --> 00:04:00.278
And this protein would bind only a receptor that
was already marked.

00:04:00.791 --> 00:04:04.946
The laboratory stated in its paper that the
retinal protein was a stand-in.

00:04:05.509 --> 00:04:08.759
Three years later, the version that acts outside
the retina was found.

00:04:09.617 --> 00:04:15.376
Martin Lohse, a fellow in the same laboratory,
screened for genes similar to retinal arrestin.

00:04:15.865 --> 00:04:19.018
He found a protein of 418 amino acids.

00:04:19.537 --> 00:04:26.827
It reduced signalling by the kinase-marked
beta-adrenergic receptor by more than 75%, and
it did not act on rhodopsin.
